    question: "What happens to the 480-a enrollment if I sell the property?",
    answer: "Under NY Real Property Tax Law § 480-a, the obligation to follow the management plan stays with the property — not the individual owner — for the remainder of the commitment period. A buyer purchasing enrolled land takes on the same commitment. Subdivisions of less than 50 acres created within the commitment period are subject to rollback taxes.",
  },
  {
    question: "How long does the 480-a application process take with NY DEC?",
    answer: "After a management plan is prepared and submitted to the DEC Regional Forester for review, approval typically takes 2 to 6 months depending on the Regional Forester's workload and property complexity. Once the DEC approves the plan, the landowner files the initial commitment form with both the town assessor and the DEC. The first property tax exemption generally applies to the assessment roll for the following tax year — meaning the benefit does not always appear in the same year the application is submitted. Environmental Forest Products coordinates the full submission process and follows up through to DEC approval.",
  },
